%I A402000 #6 Nov 16 2026 22:52:52
%S A402000 1,2,4,4,4,4,5,5,5,5,14,6,6,8,6,14,7,8,7,8,7,8,8,11,8,11,12
%N A402000 Least k such that a majority of free k-celled polyominoes contain a copy of the quasi-polyomino with binary code A401998(n).
%C A402000 Least k such that A401999(n,k) < A000105(k)/2.
%C A402000 It follows from Madras's pattern theorem for lattice clusters that lim_{k->oo} A401999(n,k)/A000105(k) = 0, so a(n) is well-defined for all n.
%H A402000 Neal Madras, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01608793">A pattern theorem for lattice clusters</a>, Annals of Combinatorics 3 (1999), 357-384; arXiv:<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/math/9902161">math/9902161</a> [math.PR], 1999.
%e A402000 For n = 18, the quasi-polyomino with binary code A401998(18) = 40 is {(2,0),(0,2)}. The number of free k-celled polyominoes that do not contain a copy of that quasi-polyomino is A401999(18,k) = A402003(k). For k < 7, A402003(k) >= A000105(k)/2, but A402003(7) = 37 < A000105(7)/2 = 108/2 = 54, so a(18) = 7.
%Y A402000 Cf. A000105, A401399, A401998, A401999, A402003.
%K A402000 nonn,more,new
%O A402000 2,2
%A A402000 _Pontus von Brömssen_, Nov 11 2026
